Problem 2A :
We have order as :
1. The good is provided, and they did not contribute (Free-riding).
2. The good is provided, and they did contribute.
3. The good is not provided, and they did not contribute.
4. The good is not provided, and they did contribute (Wasted capital).
Case 1 : There is more than k donors
If there is more than k donors, say m, then if one decides not to contribute, m-1 > k still contribute, and not contributing and good is provided is higher 
than did contribute and good is provided, so here all would decide not to contribute, implying it has no Nash Equilibria.
For anyone who was already not donating, they will continue not donating, but it doesn't matter as there have already been people that will switch.
So No Nash Equilibrium.

Case 2 : Equal to k donors 
Here, if a person who is donating chooses to stop, then the good is not provided, which is a worse outcome than good provided and money donated, so the people already
donating, will continue to do so.
If someone is not donating, they will continue not donating as not donating and good provided is best outcome. So, we can conclude that this is a Nash Equilibrium.
Thus any combination where exactly k donate should be a Nash Equilibrium

Case 3 : Less than k donors
Here also, like first case, anyone who is donating will switch to not donating as the good is not provided either way.
Again, No Nash Equilibrium, unless, to begin with, no one was contributing.
If no one was contributing to begin with, then it is a Nash Equilibrium because no one will switch to donating from not donating.

Problem 2B :
1.
Under this case, any one going for and attaining the stag is better off than going for hare.
Case 1 : More than or equal to m hunters contribute
Here, for any hunter going for stag, the payoff is better and hence they will continue helping capture the stag.
But, anyone going for the hare will switch to stag, as it is better payoff. 
Thus, no Nash Equilibrium.
There is a special case, if all the hunters choose Stag, then it is a Nash Equilibrium

Case 2 : Less than m contribute
Here, those that are going for Hare continue to do so, but those going Stag will change to Hare, so no Nash Equilibrium.

2. Updated Game
Case 1 : More than k hunters choose Stag
In this case, they get a share that is less than 1/k, so they will choose to switch to Hare rather than Stag
No Nash Equilibrium.
If there is exactly k, then those choosing Stag continue to do so, but anyone choosing Hare will not switch,
Thus, it is Nash Equilibrium if exactly k choose Stag.

Case 2 : Less than k but more than m hunters choose Stag
Hunters choosing Stag remain but hunters choosing Hare switch to Stag.
No Nash Equilibrium.
At exactly m hunters with Stag, the same holds.

Case 3 : Less than m hunters choose Stag
Anyone choosing Stag will pivot to Hare. No Nash Equilibrum.
Special case here is if no hunters choose Stag. Then, it is a Nash Equilibrium.







